{"success": true, "executor": "codex", "model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "task": "review", "duration_seconds": 211, "response": "Verdict: not publish-ready. The structure is right, but there is one broken source link, several factual overstatements, and unflagged first-person claims.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. High \u2014 Rodney\u2019s personal workflow claims are not flagged for verification.\n\n> \u201cI use a simple test: does the task require context and tradeoffs, or can a machine evaluate the result with a repeatable pass-or-fail rule?\u201d\n\n> \u201cI do not ask an agent to remember to run tests before claiming that a change works. I require a verification step to pass and the agent to observe its output.\u201d\n\n> \u201cWhenever I write \u2018always remember to\u2019 in a prompt, I ask whether the instruction belongs in a script, rule, test, or merge gate. If it does, I move it.\u201d\n\nThese are claims about Rodney\u2019s actual behavior, not general advice and not resume-verifiable. The hard rules therefore require user review/flagging, but the accompanying revision notes say \u201cVerification markers \u2014 None.\u201d Git history shows that similar claims existed in the older body, so there is no proof this revision invented them\u2014but prior copy is not the same as Rodney confirming them. Confirm them, mark them for review, or rewrite them impersonally. [Draft lines 15, 41, and 69](/Users/rodneylewis/Sprintz/Internal/rl22.github.io/.claude/worktrees/unhide-homepage-sections-b06d72/content-strategy/posts/code-over-willpower.md:15)\n\n2. High \u2014 the GitHub link is malformed, and the claim omits GitHub\u2019s bypass nuance.\n\n> \u201cGitHub protected branches and required status checks provide one implementation: named checks must pass before changes enter the protected branch.\u201d\n\nThe article URL contains `branches-and-merges/in-your-repository`; the working canonical path is `branches-and-merges-in-your-repository`. More importantly, GitHub says required checks prevent affected collaborators from merging, while admins and roles with bypass permission are exempt by default unless bypassing is explicitly disabled. Change this to something like: \u201cWhen required status checks are enabled, affected collaborators cannot merge until those checks pass.\u201d [Draft line 49](/Users/rodneylewis/Sprintz/Internal/rl22.github.io/.claude/worktrees/unhide-homepage-sections-b06d72/content-strategy/posts/code-over-willpower.md:49) [GitHub\u2019s current documentation](https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-protected-branches/about-protected-branches)\n\n3. Medium \u2014 the opening uses an unsupported number and makes an unnecessarily universal prediction.\n\n> \u201cAsk it to follow the same instruction a hundred times and the outputs will be close, not identical.\u201d\n\nNo hundred-run experiment is cited, so this sounds like a measured result that never happened. \u201cWill \u2026 not identical\u201d is also too absolute: behavior depends on model settings, system implementation, tools, and caching. This is the draft\u2019s only fabricated-number risk. Safer: \u201cRun the same instruction repeatedly and the outputs may vary.\u201d [Draft line 1](/Users/rodneylewis/Sprintz/Internal/rl22.github.io/.claude/worktrees/unhide-homepage-sections-b06d72/content-strategy/posts/code-over-willpower.md:1)\n\n4. Medium \u2014 the draft overstates the determinism of executable checks.\n\n> \u201cThey need only answer a narrow question the same way every time.\u201d\n\nTests, link checkers, and CI jobs can be flaky, environment-dependent, or incorrectly specified. Code makes a rule executable; it does not automatically make the result invariant or correct. \u201cWell-designed checks evaluate a narrow condition reproducibly\u201d would preserve the point without pretending nondeterministic tests do not exist. [Draft line 9](/Users/rodneylewis/Sprintz/Internal/rl22.github.io/.cla